3167: \section{Gate summary}
3168: 
3169: \subsection{Case-study witness protocol}
3170: 
3171: Table~\ref{tab:gates} lists the deterministic gates used by
3172: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  The rows are operational
3173: interfaces: each gate has an evidence surface and a failure class it is meant
3174: to prevent.
3175: 
3176: \begin{table}
3177: \caption{Deterministic gate interfaces}
3178: \label{tab:gates}
3179: \small
3180: \begin{tabular}{p{0.23\linewidth}p{0.33\linewidth}p{0.32\linewidth}}
3181: \toprule
3182: Gate & Evidence surface & Failure prevented \\
3183: \midrule
3184: Formal-source gate & Lean build, axiom checks, axiom-purity audits & broken or impure formal targets \\
3185: Traceability gate & declaration inventory, source maps, marker audits & stale paper labels and missing source facts \\
3186: Discovery gate & certificate ledger, demotion, lineage DAG & statement-only, code-only, or mechanical ``discoveries'' \\
3187: Theorem-content delta gate & theorem inventory, phase-D hard lint, delta inventory & file churn or compilation reported as substantive theorem growth \\
3188: Artifact-limitation gate & status files, limitation ledgers, rerun logs when present & drift from bounded artifact evidence to stronger dynamic claims \\
3189: Scheduler gate & critical-path scoring and backlog windows & duplicated or random agent dispatch \\
3190: Promotion gate & active \texttt{2026\_*} directory plus pipeline metadata & intake seeds entering the daemon prematurely \\
3191: Overlap gate & route identity, semantic-overlap ledgers, submitted-sibling paths, board state & duplicate or racing paper routes \\
3192: Submission-state gate & submission markers, venue state, board state, submission metadata & invalid advancement of active or submitted routes \\
3193: Submission-pack gate & review, venue, bibliography, and metadata checks & unreviewed or under-specified submission packs \\
3194: \bottomrule
3195: \end{tabular}
3196: \end{table}
3197: 
3198: \begin{lemma}[Gate-witness criterion]
3199: \label{lem:gate-witness-criterion}
3200: A case record is a historical path-verified witness for the safety interface
3201: when it names the source or artifact snapshot surface, the invariant that failed,
3202: the gate decision recorded for that snapshot, and the safe manuscript lesson.
3203: Under the finite-record interface theorem,
3204: Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}, such a witness can support a
3205: case-study claim about the recorded historical gate surface, but not a stronger
3206: command-run, benchmark, live-daemon, rebuild, dynamic-artifact, or semantic
3207: validation claim, unless the corresponding command, source state, environment,
3208: exit code, and log path are also recorded.
3209: \end{lemma}
3210: 
3211: \begin{proof}
3212: The four named fields are exactly the data required by the definition of a
3213: path-verified historical evidence level: they identify the snapshot surface on
3214: which the case is checked, which gate predicate was evaluated, what decision was
3215: recorded, and which bounded claim may be carried into the manuscript.  A live
3216: benchmark, daemon execution, full-source rebuild, dynamic artifact run, or
3217: semantic validator would add an event time, command, source state, environment,
3218: exit code, log path, and usually a new artifact-output surface.  Those fields are
3219: not read by a historical witness.  Therefore a witness record with only the four
3220: path-verified fields supports the bounded case-study claim and cannot be used as
3221: evidence for a fresh command-run assertion.\end{proof}
3222: 
3223: \begin{proposition}[Path-complete case witnesses]
3224: \label{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses}
3225: Suppose a case-study row is used as load-bearing evidence for
3226: the finite-record interface theorem, Theorem~\ref{thm:publication-safety-interface}.  If the row is path-complete in
3227: the sense above, then the row supplies exactly the evidence surface required for
3228: a path-verified historical snapshot case claim and no evidence surface for a
3229: fresh daemon, full-source, theorem-content, artifact-suite, benchmark, or
3230: semantic-substrate rerun.  In fact every accepted path-complete case row factors
3231: through one of four finite witness skeletons---promotion, route/submission
3232: blocking, theorem-content non-growth, or artifact-limitation disclosure---and its
3233: admissible manuscript lesson is the unique bounded lesson attached to that
3234: skeleton.  Replacing that lesson by a fresh experiment, dynamic validation, live
3235: benchmark, or semantic artifact claim changes the skeleton by adding a
3236: command-run or artifact-semantic coordinate, so the original row no longer
3237: certifies the stronger claim.  In
3238: particular, a promotion row is sufficient only
3239: for the bounded claim that an intake packet is not daemon-visible publication
3240: work before human promotion; a route-overlap row is sufficient only for the
3241: bounded claim that the overlap and submission-state gates blocked advancement
3242: until one of the recorded next actions occurs; a theorem-content row is
3243: sufficient only for the bounded claim that file churn or compilation did not
3244: show substantive theorem growth; and an artifact-limitation row is sufficient
3245: only for the bounded claim that the artifact limitation must be disclosed or must
3246: block promotion.
3247: \end{proposition}
3248: 
3249: \begin{proof}
3250: Let $r$ be a path-complete case row.  By definition, every load-bearing
3251: component of $r$ has a concrete source or artifact snapshot path, directory
3252: manifest, or explicit historical boundary.  The promotion interface names the
3253: candidate surface, human-promotion evidence, active-paper
3254: markers, gate decision, and the bounded manuscript claim; these fields are
3255: precisely the data needed to distinguish inactive intake from publication work.
3256: The route-overlap interface names the active route, the submitted sibling that
3257: creates the chronology constraint, the reason for overlap, the gate decision,
3258: and the finite set of permitted next actions.  These are precisely the data
3259: needed to check whether advancement is allowed without appealing to generated
3260: prose, and the submission-state evidence prevents a route from racing an already
3261: submitted sibling.  The theorem-content interface names the changed surface,
3262: the theorem inventory or hard-lint surface, the substantive-content delta, the
3263: gate decision, and the allowed manuscript claim; these fields are precisely the
3264: data needed to reject extension claims based only on file churn or successful
3265: compilation.  The artifact-limitation interface names the status file, the
3266: limitation ledger, the observed limitation, the gate decision, and the only
3267: manuscript claim that may be carried forward.  These are precisely the data
3268: needed to prevent a failed or bounded artifact check from being promoted into a
3269: stronger dynamic-validation claim.  Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion} then
3270: applies to each such row:
3271: the named paths give the source surface, the interface fields give the failed
3272: invariant and decision, and the last field gives the safe lesson.  The same
3273: lemma also rules out upgrading the row to a command-run claim unless the extra
3274: command-run fields are recorded.  In particular, the proof does not rerun the
3275: publication daemon, overlap scanner, theorem-content detector, artifact suite,
3276: or GroundCompiler evaluator; it proves only that the historical path surfaces are
3277: sufficient for the bounded lessons displayed in the table.
3278: 
3279: It remains to justify the skeleton and uniqueness assertions.  The operational
3280: interface dispatches each case row by its gate family.  A row whose required gate
3281: is promotion has exactly the promotion skeleton; a row whose required gates are
3282: overlap and submission-state has the route/submission-blocking skeleton; a row
3283: whose required gate is theorem-content has the non-growth skeleton; and a row
3284: whose required gates are status and limitation-ledger has the artifact-limitation
3285: skeleton.  These four skeletons are disjoint because their gate sets differ, and
3286: the row's safe-lesson field is part of the finite record checked by
3287: Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion}.  Hence, once the skeleton is fixed, the
3288: only accepted lesson is the bounded lesson printed in that row.  A fresh
3289: experiment or dynamic validation would require the command-run boundary fields in
3290: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}; an artifact-semantic claim
3291: would require an additional artifact pass record or semantic evaluator surface.
3292: Those coordinates are not read by the path-complete row and are not present in
3293: the four skeletons.  The stronger reading is therefore rejected by
3294: Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-coordinate-determination-boundary} together with the
3295: finite obstruction skeleton, while the original bounded historical lesson remains
3296: accepted.\end{proof}
3297: 
3298: \section{Case studies}
3299: 
3300: Each row of Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses} is a witness in the sense of
3301: Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion} and
3302: Proposition~\ref{prop:path-complete-case-witnesses}: source or artifact paths,
3303: failed invariant, gate decision, and safe manuscript lesson are all explicit.
3304: The reviewer-facing path catalogue is the finite manifest row
3305: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json:case_evidence} together with the
3306: snapshots under \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/}.  Newmath intake isolation
3307: uses the bundled intake-seed manifest and the bundled active-paper-detector
3308: snapshot, copied from the provenance paths
3309: \path{automath/papers/publication/newmath_intake/seeds/bedc_automation_pipeline}
3310: and \path{automath/papers/publication/pipeline_auto.py}.  The upper-fibers
3311: route-overlap record uses bundled snapshots for the route identity, submitted
3312: sibling marker, overlap ledgers \path{semantic_overlap_blockers.json} and
3313: \path{semantic_overlap_blockers.md}, and the program-board state.  The
3314: fake-extension case uses the bundled \path{phase_d_lint.py} snapshot and this
3315: paper's submitted \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md}.  The Rule110 limitation record
3316: uses bundled snapshots corresponding to \path{rule110/STATUS.md},
3317: \path{rule110/docs/collision_audit_findings.md},
3318: \path{rule110/docs/exhaustiveness_audit_findings.md}, and
3319: \path{rule110/docs/papers_research_report.md}.  Machine-local absolute paths, if
3320: present in the manifest, are provenance fields only; the load-bearing review
3321: surface is the submitted bundle path or the submitted-package path named here.
3322: 
3323: 
3324: \paragraph{Case-evidence protocol.}
3325: The four case-study rows below are not additional mathematical theorems; they
3326: are the path-verified protocol records used to instantiate
3327: Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion}.  A row is complete for this purpose when
3328: it names the source or artifact surface, the invariant that failed, the gate
3329: decision, and the safe manuscript lesson.  For the present package the row
3330: protocol is:
3331: \begin{itemize}
3332: \item \emph{Newmath intake isolation}: the intake-seed path and active-paper
3333: detector show that a candidate remains inactive preparation until human
3334: promotion creates \path{2026_*}, \path{main.tex}, and \path{PIPELINE.md}.
3335: \item \emph{Upper-fibers overlap block}: the route identity, submitted sibling,
3336: overlap ledgers, and program-board state show the recorded
3337: \path{deferred_wait_for_prior_submission} decision and the finite next-action
3338: set of closure, merge, supersession, or waiting for prior-route feedback.
3339: \item \emph{Fake-extension block}: the phase-D hard-lint script and
3340: this paper's artifact inventory show a theorem-content-gate rejection of file
3341: churn or compilation that did not add load-bearing theorem content.
3342: \item \emph{Rule110 limitation gate}: the status file, collision-audit ledger,
3343: exhaustiveness ledger, and paper-data reference show a bounded limitation that
3344: must be disclosed or must block artifact claims; they do not show fresh dynamic
3345: validation.
3346: \end{itemize}
3347: Thus the rows prove path completeness and reviewability only in the operational
3348: sense of Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion}; they do not certify fresh
3349: daemon execution, full-source rebuilds, or Rule110 reruns.
3350: 
3351: \begin{proposition}[Review-bundle case-snapshot coverage]
3352: \label{prop:review-bundle-case-snapshot-coverage}
3353: For the four case-study rows in Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}, the review
3354: bundle contains a finite external-review surface for every local case path used
3355: by the paper.  The Newmath block has two local paths, the upper-fibers block has
3356: five local paths, the fake-extension block has two local paths, and the Rule110
3357: block has four local paths, for thirteen local-path strings after resolving this
3358: paper's relative \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md} to its absolute path in the paper
3359: directory.  The manifest file
3360: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json} has one
3361: \path{case_evidence} entry for each case row and, inside each entry, a
3362: \path{reviewable_snapshots} list matching those local paths.  File-valued local
3363: surfaces are copied into \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/}; directory-valued
3364: local surfaces are represented there by directory file-manifests.  Consequently
3365: the case-study claim made by this paper is externally reviewable at the
3366: path-verified snapshot level.  It is not a command-run validation claim, a fresh
3367: full-source rebuild claim, a daemon-execution claim, or a fresh Rule110 dynamic
3368: rerun claim.
3369: \end{proposition}
3370: 
3371: \begin{proof}
3372: The path catalogue displayed at the beginning of this section is finite and
3373: partitions the case surfaces into the four named rows: two paths for Newmath
3374: intake isolation, five paths for the upper-fibers overlap block, two paths for
3375: the fake-extension block, and four paths for the Rule110 limitation gate.  The
3376: manifest row for each case repeats exactly the same local-path list in its
3377: \path{local_paths} field and attaches a \path{reviewable_snapshots} list.  In
3378: that list the Newmath row names
3379: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/newmath_intake_seed_directory_manifest.json}
3380: and \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/newmath_pipeline_auto.py}; the
3381: upper-fibers row names
3382: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/upper_fibers_route_directory_manifest.json},
3383: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/upper_fibers_SUBMITTED},
3384: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/upper_fibers_semantic_overlap_blockers.json},
3385: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/upper_fibers_semantic_overlap_blockers.md},
3386: and \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/program_board_machine.md}; the
3387: fake-extension row names
3388: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/fake_extension_phase_d_lint.py} and
3389: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/fake_extension_artifact_inventory.md}; and the
3390: Rule110 row names \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_STATUS.md},
3391: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_collision_audit_findings.md},
3392: \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_exhaustiveness_audit_findings.md},
3393: and \path{review_bundle/case_snapshots/rule110_papers_research_report.md}.
3394: These thirteen bundle paths are in bijection with the thirteen local-path
3395: strings.  The two directory-valued local paths are not treated as opaque private
3396: folders: their bundle entries are directory file-manifests, so a reviewer can
3397: inspect the directory boundary and file list used by the row.  The remaining
3398: eleven entries are file snapshots.  Thus the review surface no longer depends on
3399: bare absolute paths alone.
3400: 
3401: The proposition's negative boundary follows from the same manifest entries and
3402: from Lemma~\ref{lem:gate-witness-criterion}.  The \path{boundary} field for each
3403: case records historical or path-verified evidence and explicitly withholds fresh
3404: daemon, full-source command, and Rule110 dynamic-rerun claims.  A directory
3405: manifest or copied file snapshot supplies reviewability of the cited surface; it
3406: does not supply command, commit, environment, exit-code, and log-path fields.
3407: Therefore the conclusion is exactly path-verified snapshot reviewability and not
3408: any stronger execution claim.\end{proof}
